At Medium level, the same subject often appears in two or three paragraphs. For example, "coins" might be mentioned in several places. The question's type word decides which paragraph is correct:

"the origin of" → where it began;

"a disadvantage of" → a problem;

"a challenge to a common belief" → a correction of what people usually think.

Before answering, locate every paragraph that mentions the subject, then choose the one that matches the type word.

Psychology passages mix three kinds of content:

theories: explanations of why something happens ("one view is that...");

findings: results of studies ("researchers found that...");

recommendations: practical advice ("one strategy is to...").

Questions usually specify which kind they want ("a theory about...", "the result of an experiment...", "a practical suggestion..."). A paragraph that discusses the right topic but offers a different kind of content is a trap.

Research passages often describe an experiment in three parts: what was done (method), what happened (result) and what it means (interpretation). Questions may target any one of these: "a description of an experiment in which..." → method;

"a finding that..." → result;

"a suggestion about why..." → interpretation.

The three parts sometimes fall in different paragraphs.

This final Matching Information set combines the main Hard-level skills:

abstract wording;

several paragraphs on the same subject;

paragraphs with no answers;

letters used twice;

questions that target a paragraph's function (cause, evidence, criticism, proposal). For each question, decide the type word and find the sentence that fits it. Do not rely on topic matches.
